**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the full compositional-variation, feldspar, and selected solid-solution sections; [Mineral names and classification reports — IMA-CNMNC](../sources/IMA-CNMNC-Mineral-Names-and-Reports.lingua#r_1B4ZWGSZKGP6JM3NKPY0D5RJXH), the full relevant group/nomenclature report; [HPx-eos and THERMOCALC — models, data, and interpretation](../sources/HPx-eos-and-THERMOCALC.lingua#r_0QYHHGNWRA8RQEM5MADWXDRQFJ), the selected solution-model definition and primary paper.

Solid solution changes site occupants within a structural family over an allowed range. Site size/coordination, charge, temperature, pressure, ordering, and thermodynamic interactions influence the range and behavior. A linear interpolation of one property is an empirical/model assumption rather than a definition of solid solution.

Coupled substitutions preserve constraints through linked changes. In a simplified feldspar account, Na⁺ + Si⁴⁺ ↔ Ca²⁺ + Al³⁺ preserves total charge while changing different sites. Merely replacing Na with Ca without accounting for the other change does not describe the same electrically balanced framework.

Endmembers are reference compositions under a chosen model. A measured mineral can require several endmembers and a site-allocation scheme; the same bulk composition may admit more than one ordering or oxidation-state account. State how total iron and unmeasured components are treated before calculating an endmember fraction.

**Work:** derive two balanced substitutions from complete mineral-group treatments, checking site capacity and charge separately. Convert a simple analyzed composition into candidate endmember proportions and compare an alternative allocation. Identify observations that could distinguish oxidation state, ordering, vacancies, or a mixed analysis.

Read the complete compositional-variation and group-specific sections. Later activities, nonideal mixing, exsolution, and thermometry use these structural/chemical definitions; a convenient formula normalization should not silently determine the physical model used in a petrological calculation.

**Continue:** [Relate defects and ordering to properties without confusing them with a new bulk composition](Defects-ordering-and-exsolution.lingua#r_1TAP18152580TY3JWTWDJ3XE2T).

